\title{Adding an Antichain: Strict Monotonicity of the Boolean-Lattice Extremal Numbers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{6 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
For a finite poset $P$, let $\La(n,P)$ denote the largest size of a family
$\FF \subseteq 2^{[n]}$ containing no (weak) copy of $P$, and $\LaS(n,P)$ the analogous
quantity for strong copies. We study $P = B_d$, the Boolean lattice on $d$ atoms, and
isolate a single structural mechanism governing how these quantities grow with $d$.

The mechanism is a \emph{lifting lemma}: for every antichain $A$ of $B_{d+1}$ there is an
order embedding of $B_d$ into $B_{d+1}$ whose image is disjoint from $A$. Such embeddings
are indexed by the up-sets of $B_d$; the up-set generated by $A$ produces one that dodges
$A$. From this we deduce the Antichain Union Theorem (a $B_d$-free family united with an
antichain is $B_{d+1}$-free, for weak and for strong copies); the Height Sandwich (height
$\le d$ forces $B_d$-freeness, $B_d$-freeness forces height $\le 2^d-1$, both thresholds
sharp); the Strict Monotonicity Theorem ($\La(n,B_d) < \La(n,B_{d+1})$ if and only if
$d \le n$, and identically for $\LaS$); and the Pigeonhole Gain Theorem
($2^n + n\La(n,B_d) \le (n+1)\La(n,B_{d+1})$ for all $n,d$). We give exhaustive small-case
data, algorithms, and a subadditivity conjecture of which the Antichain Union Theorem is
the first case.
\end{abstract}

